  "evidence": "TEXT_C: Big NHS and social care expansions (end privatisation, universal NHS dentistry, mental health within 28 days, free personal care, disability benefit uplifts); major anti‑poverty and housing measures (150,000 social homes/year, rent controls, end no‑fault evictions, raise Universal Credit, scrap two‑child cap); redistribution and “polluter pays” moves (wealth and CGT reforms, remove NI upper limit, carbon tax to ~£500/t); equality of rights and voice (PR, scrap voter ID, defend HRA/ECHR, humane migration and asylum policies). TEXT_F: Reward/discipline framing (NI cuts, tougher welfare sanctions, fraud bill); authority/loyalty measures (mandatory National Service, tougher sentencing, more prisons, Rwanda removals, annual cap on migration); some care provisions (NHS staffing and funding uplift, childcare expansion); culture/identity rules (biological sex in Equality Act).",
  "reasoning": "The electorate profile weights Care highest and Equality next, and TEXT_C prioritizes expansive health, social care, housing and anti‑poverty measures alongside rights and political equality, while still invoking proportionality via polluter‑pays and fair‑tax reforms. TEXT_F leans more on proportionality through conditional welfare and on Authority/Loyalty via national service, policing and border controls—foundations that carry less weight in the given profile—while offering comparatively narrower Care and Equality commitments.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_C"
}
